 "authorLine": "Agent run r7, 2026-10-25",
 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: across the 553 volumes indexed, the series prints the Department of State's own reasoning on recognizing Latin American governments that came to power by force from Seward's refusal to recognize Maximilian (1865) to Carter's isolation of García Meza (1980): about 1,100 documents pair the word recognition with a coup, junta, de facto or provisional government inside Latin American chapters, and sixty-three chapter headings name a recognition question outright. They show one durable test — control of the country, no active resistance, and the declared intention to meet international obligations — and four overlays that changed what Washington asked: Wilson's demand for constitutional legitimacy and observed elections (1913–1920), the Central American treaty rule of 1923 against coup governments (adopted 1923, abandoned 1936), wartime consultation among the republics with an anti-Axis test (1943–1948), and the Cold War's anti-communist assurances with, under Kennedy, dated elections and civil liberties (1955–1963). After 1964 the documents show relations simply continuing or recognition following consultation, with a deliberate drift toward recognizing states rather than judging governments. Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed documents came overwhelmingly from the Department's Central Decimal File (classes such as 812.00, Mexico political affairs, and 816.01, El Salvador government) and, after 1961, from the Kennedy, Johnson, Nixon and Carter national security files; the editors' footnotes point unprinted to the Executive Secretariat's lot files, the Bureau of Inter-American Affairs' office files and the OAS files listed in the archival sources block. What this request cannot get from FRUS: the text of the 1923 Central American treaty as a document (only its Article II is quoted; the 1907 convention is printed inside the delegate's report), any general policy paper on coups from the 1960s (only the country cases), the Latin American governments' own doctrines (Tobar, Estrada, Betancourt) except as reported by American officers, and anything on the coups of 1981–1988, for which the one South America volume indexed has no recognition discussion.",
